  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 138 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Analysis shows strong Bermuda high slowly backing to the \neast as an upper trough is carving itself out. This upper trough \nwill deepen into an upper low, and this will set the stage for muggy \nconditions with hit-or-miss shower and thunderstorm activity. Warm, \nmoist advection is already producing some showers around Lake \nOntario as moisture rounds a 500mb ridge axis along coastal New \nEngland. Increasingly humid conditions will push dewpoints towards \n70 as the day wears on. An incoming trough will provide a focus for \nshowers and storms mid-morning through this evening. With no capping \nin place, activity will likely get underway across northern New \nYork, maybe even by the time this is published, but most likely mid \nto late this morning. Temperatures will climb into the 80s by \nafternoon, and this will generate plenty of surface-based \ninstability, especially across northern New York. The benefit to \nhaving no capping is that early convection will produce plenty of \nclouds that will extend northeast in 50-60 knot flow in the upper \nlevels, which will limit solar heating and could prevent heat \nindices from creeping into the mid 90s. Still with heat indices in \nthe mid 80s to lower 90s, it will feel a bit uncomfortable, today. \nSo seek shade and drink plenty of water if doing prolonged \nactivities. \n\nThis warmth and humidity will help bring PWATs to near 2\". Combined \nwith 1000-1500 J/kg of CAPE and trough providing some focus, some \nheavy downpours will be likely. A few thunderstorms may be strong. 0-\n6km bulk shear values are at the bottom threshold for marginal \norganization (~25 kts), and the newer RRFS suggests some clustering \nis possible. Sounding profiles match schematics for wet microbursts, \nand so any stronger thunderstorm may produce gusty winds. However, \nthis will be conditional upon whether mid-morning to early afternoon \nconvection may limit instability through increased cloud cover.\n\nPrecipitation chances will dwindle tonight, but not completely hit \nzero with very moist conditions in place. Dewpoints remain high \nbehind the trough, and so nighttime lows in the mid 60s to lower 70s \nis expected. Friday's conditions will be similar with 80s across the \nregion. For tomorrow, there won't be quite as much precipitation due \nto a lack of a trough, and so we may have a better shot at \napproaching mid 90s heat indices, but will give things time to see \nhow dewpoints trend over the course of today. By Friday evening \nthrough Saturday night, a messy conglomerate of troughs will angle \nnortheast. Even with the loss of daytime heating, some showers and a \nfew rumbles could still take place during the overnight hours. Each \nday should get a little bit cooler at the very least.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The start of next week will feature seasonably \nwarm conditions, although less humid compared to previous days \nas the Bermuda high weakens and westerly flows prevails across \nthe region. Highs at the start of the week will be in the upper \n70s to mid 80s, but by the middle of the week they will drop a \nfew degrees to be in the lower 70s and low 80s. A cold front \nlooks to move through the region sometime late Monday into \nTuesday, bringing widespread showers and a few possible \nthunderstorms, although the timing of the boundary will have a \nlarge influence on any convective elements. Towards the middle \nof next week, guidance suggests that drier conditions return, \nalthough we'll have to see as we get closer.",
